import Foundation

// "Go deeper" lessons for chapters 1–10, keyed by chapter number.
// Introductory wording — have community educators review before release.

enum ChapterLessons {
    static let byChapter: [Int: [Lesson]] = [
        1: [
            Lesson(heading: "Faith in the marketplace", symbol: "storefront.fill", body: [
                "Islam was born in a trading town, and the Prophet himself worked as a merchant. From the start, Muslim ethics spoke to everyday life: earn honestly, keep promises, pay fair wages, and share with those in need.",
                "The Imams have warned against two opposite mistakes: withdrawing from the world as if it had no spiritual value, and chasing the world as if it were all that mattered. Din and dunya held in balance means working well, living well and remembering God throughout."
            ], reflection: "Where in my ordinary week do faith and work already meet?"),
            Lesson(heading: "Quality of life as a duty", symbol: "heart.text.square.fill", body: [
                "A recurring idea in the Imams' guidance is that improving the quality of human life is an ethical duty — not only for the community, but for the wider societies it lives among.",
                "The earth and its resources are held as a trust (amana). Using them wisely, and leaving the world better for the next generation, is part of living one's faith in the world."
            ])
        ],
        2: [
            Lesson(heading: "Three levels of soul", symbol: "leaf.fill", body: [
                "Classical Muslim philosophers, including Ismaili thinkers, described a ladder of life. Plants have a growing soul: they feed and grow. Animals add a sensing soul: they see, feel and move. Humans add a rational, 'speaking' soul (nafs-i natiqa) that can think, reason and know God.",
                "Nasir Khusraw saw creation rising in stages — minerals, plants, animals, then human beings at the summit of the physical world. We share growth with plants and senses with animals, but the rational soul is what makes the spiritual journey possible."
            ], reflection: "What do I feed my rational soul each day, the way I feed my body?"),
            Lesson(heading: "The intellect must be trained", symbol: "figure.walk", body: [
                "Intellect is a gift, but like a muscle it grows through use. Ismaili thinkers stressed disciplined, repeated study of both the physical world and spiritual questions.",
                "Knowledge is described as the soul's nourishment. Just as a body without food weakens, a soul without knowledge stays small. Learning is therefore not only useful — it is a spiritual act."
            ])
        ],
        3: [
            Lesson(heading: "Why both sides matter", symbol: "scale.3d", body: [
                "Classical writers often compared zahir and batin to a body and its soul. A body without a soul is lifeless; a soul in this world needs a body to act.",
                "Someone who holds only to the outer form can miss its purpose. Someone who throws away the outer form loses the doorway to the meaning. The tradition asks us to keep both, and to let the inner meaning give life to the outer practice."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "Reading nature as a book", symbol: "mountain.2.fill", body: [
                "The Qur'an repeatedly calls the natural world 'signs' (ayat) — the same word used for its own verses. Day and night, rain and growth, the stars: all are signs for people who reflect.",
                "So the habit of looking beyond the surface is not limited to scripture. A sunrise has a zahir (light and physics) and a batin (what it teaches about renewal and guidance)."
            ], reflection: "Pick one thing in nature you saw today. What could its inner meaning be?")
        ],
        4: [
            Lesson(heading: "Who interprets?", symbol: "person.fill.questionmark", body: [
                "If scripture has inner meanings, who can say what they are? In Shia thought the answer is the rightful guide: Hazrat Ali and the Imams after him, who are seen as the custodians of the Qur'an's meaning.",
                "Historically, Ismaili teachers (da'is) taught ta'wil in stages, step by step, as a student's understanding grew. Ta'wil was never meant as personal guesswork, but as teaching connected to the Imam of the time."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "An example: the Verse of Light", symbol: "light.max", body: [
                "The Qur'an says that God is the light of the heavens and the earth, and describes that light as a lamp within a glass, lit from a blessed tree (24:35).",
                "Ismaili commentators have read this verse as pointing to the light of guidance carried by the Prophet and the Imams — a light that illuminates the hearts and minds of seekers. The outward image is a lamp; the inner meaning is guidance."
            ], reflection: "What does 'light' mean to me beyond what my eyes can see?")
        ],
        5: [
            Lesson(heading: "Ghadir Khumm", symbol: "flag.fill", body: [
                "In 632 CE, returning from his final pilgrimage, the Prophet stopped at a place called Ghadir Khumm. Raising Hazrat Ali's hand, he declared: 'Of whomsoever I am mawla, Ali is his mawla.'",
                "Shia Muslims understand this as the designation of Ali as the Prophet's successor in spiritual authority — the starting point of the Imamat."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "Visible and concealed", symbol: "eye.slash.fill", body: [
                "The Imamat has continued without break, but not always in public view. At times of danger the Imams lived in concealment (satr), guiding through trusted teachers. At other times they ruled openly, as the Fatimid Imam-caliphs did.",
                "Teachers called da'is and pirs carried the message far from the Imam's residence. In South Asia, pirs such as Pir Sadr al-Din composed devotional hymns called ginans that are still recited today."
            ], reflection: "How has guidance reached me — through which people and teachers?")
        ],
        6: [
            Lesson(heading: "Early-morning meditation", symbol: "moon.stars.fill", body: [
                "Many Ismailis practise silent meditation in the quiet hours before dawn, often called bandagi. It is a personal, inward practice of remembrance.",
                "The stillness of those hours is valued because the mind is quieter and the world less distracting — a time for the soul to turn toward its source."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "Poetry as remembrance", symbol: "music.note", body: [
                "Devotional poetry is a living part of Ismaili practice. In South Asia, ginans composed by the pirs are recited in many languages. In Central Asia, qasidas and poems attributed to Nasir Khusraw are sung.",
                "These poems carry teachings about the soul, ethics and love of God in a form that is easy to remember and moves the heart as well as the mind."
            ], reflection: "Is there a ginan, qasida or verse that stays with me? Why that one?")
        ],
        7: [
            Lesson(heading: "A history of curiosity", symbol: "scroll.fill", body: [
                "In the 10th century, the Brethren of Purity (Ikhwan al-Safa') wrote an encyclopedia of 52 epistles covering mathematics, astronomy, music, natural science and philosophy. Many scholars link them to an Ismaili milieu.",
                "Two centuries later, the astronomer Nasir al-Din al-Tusi lived among the Nizari Ismailis of Persia, using their libraries. There he wrote the Akhlaq-i Nasiri, a famous work on ethics, dedicated to an Ismaili governor."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "Creation in stages", symbol: "arrow.up.forward", body: [
                "In Ismaili cosmology, God originates creation by command, and the world then unfolds through stages: minerals, then plants, then animals, then human beings.",
                "This is not the same as modern evolutionary biology, which works through natural selection over millions of years. But it shows a tradition comfortable with the idea that creation develops over time — which is why many Ismailis find no conflict with science."
            ], reflection: "Can wonder at how something works deepen my sense of who made it?")
        ],
        8: [
            Lesson(heading: "Capacity and responsibility", symbol: "person.crop.circle.badge.checkmark", body: [
                "The Qur'an teaches that God does not burden any soul beyond what it can bear (2:286). Divine justice measures each person by their own capacity and circumstances.",
                "A person with a disability or a different way of thinking is not 'less' in the eyes of God. Each soul's journey is its own, and is judged with perfect fairness."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "Building an inclusive jamat", symbol: "figure.roll", body: [
                "Inclusion is practical: ramps and seating, quiet spaces for those who need them, clear signage, patient volunteers, and programmes designed so everyone can join.",
                "It is also about attitude — noticing who is missing from a gathering and asking what would help them take part."
            ], reflection: "Who might find it hard to join in at my jamatkhana or school, and what could change?")
        ],
        9: [
            Lesson(heading: "From Persia to the world", symbol: "airplane", body: [
                "In the 1840s, Aga Khan I moved from Persia to India, and Bombay became the centre of the Imamat. Under Aga Khan III, Ismaili communities grew across South Asia and East Africa.",
                "In 1972, the expulsion of Asians from Uganda forced many Ismaili families to rebuild their lives, especially in Canada and the United Kingdom. Today Ismailis live across Asia, Africa, Europe and North America."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "Buildings that speak", symbol: "building.2.fill", body: [
                "Ismaili Centres in cities such as London, Vancouver, Lisbon, Dubai, Dushanbe and Toronto are places for prayer, but also for public lectures, exhibitions and dialogue with the wider society.",
                "Their architecture deliberately blends Islamic traditions with local styles — a visible sign of a community that is rooted in its faith and at home in the places it lives."
            ])
        ],
        10: [
            Lesson(heading: "Pluralism in history", symbol: "building.columns.fill", body: [
                "Fatimid Cairo was a diverse city. People of different faiths — Sunni and Shia Muslims, Christians and Jews — served in the Fatimid administration, some at its highest levels.",
                "Pluralism is therefore not a modern import for Ismailis; it has deep roots in their own history."
            ]),
            Lesson(heading: "Service as a spiritual practice", symbol: "hand.raised.fill", body: [
                "Khidmat is not only helpful work — it is understood as an offering. Giving one's time, skills and knowledge for others is a way of expressing gratitude to God.",
                "Volunteers organise jamatkhana life, run religious education, support health and education programmes, and respond in emergencies. Much of what the community does rests on this spirit."
            ], reflection: "What skill do I have that could serve someone else this month?")
        ]
    ]
}
